<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="UTF-8"/><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width"/><meta name="description" content="Cycle racing in the 2000s: Armstrong, Landis and Rasmussen, carbon bicycles, power measurement, ten speeds and Shimano Di2."/><meta property="og:image" content="https://teob.dk/images/periods/2000-2009/allos-2000.webp"/><title>When cycling became a control room · 2000–2009 · The Evolution of Bikes</title><link rel="stylesheet" href={`${base}/styles/global.css`}/></head><body><EnglishHeader current="periods" danishPath="/perioder/2000-2009/"/><main><article class="wildwest-page decade1970-page">
<header class="wildwest-hero decade1940-hero"><div><p class="section-label">The period 2000–2009</p><h1>When cycling became<br/><em>a control room</em></h1></div><p>Training, nutrition and bicycles were measured ever more closely. So was doping. The result was greater speed, specialisation and a sport losing control of its own story.</p></header><figure class="decade1970-hero-image"><img src={`${images}/allos-2000.webp`} alt="The Tour peloton climbing Col d’Allos in 2000"/><figcaption>The peloton on Col d’Allos in 2000. Photo: Lazz06 · CC BY-SA 4.0.</figcaption></figure>
<section class="wildwest-lead decade1940-lead"><p class="section-label">The plan before the race</p><p>A Tour was now prepared all year: routes surveyed, climbs measured, daily equipment selected and riders kept within increasingly narrow figures.</p></section><section class="era-facts decade1970-facts">{facts.map(([n,l])=><div><strong>{n}</strong><span>{l}</span></div>)}</section>
<section class="wildwest-copy"><div><p class="section-label">Armstrong and Bruyneel</p><h2>Professionalism with a darker side</h2></div><div><p>Lance Armstrong and Johan Bruyneel placed the Tour at the centre of a strict programme of camps, reconnaissance, aerodynamic tests and weight control. Many methods already existed, but US Postal combined them visibly — and won on the road from 1999 to 2005.</p><p>In 2012 Armstrong’s results from August 1998 onwards were annulled. USADA described an extensive, organised doping programme. The work was meticulous and modern; so was the cheating.</p></div></section>
<section class="decade1970-mechanics"><div><p class="section-label">A change of material</p><h2>Carbon becomes normal</h2><p>Aluminium, steel, titanium and carbon coexisted early in the decade. By its end, carbon dominated top-level racing. Deep wheels became specialist tools, compact chainsets widened gearing, and leading groups reached ten rear sprockets.</p></div><aside><span>Carbon frames</span><span>10 sprockets</span><span>Power meters</span><span>Wind tunnels</span><span>Deep rims</span><span>Di2</span></aside></section>
<section class="era-gallery"><figure class="gallery-tall"><img src={`${images}/armstrong-alpe-dhuez.webp`} alt="Lance Armstrong climbing Alpe d’Huez in 2001"/><figcaption>Armstrong on Alpe d’Huez. Photo: Petrusbarbygere · public domain.</figcaption></figure><figure><img src={`${images}/ullrich-gaillac.webp`} alt="Jan Ullrich in the 2003 Tour time trial"/><figcaption>Jan Ullrich at Gaillac. Photo: BerndtF · CC BY-SA 3.0.</figcaption></figure><figure><img src={`${images}/sastre-2008.webp`} alt="Carlos Sastre in yellow in Paris in 2008"/><figcaption>Sastre in yellow. Photo: Bjarte Hetland · CC BY 3.0.</figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="wildwest-copy dark"><div><p class="section-label">Watts over instinct</p><h2>The body becomes data</h2></div><div><p>Power meters showed the rider’s actual output. With heart rate and laboratory tests, training became easier to control. Teams planned carbohydrate, recovery and hydration more closely. Figures improved preparation, but the same medical and logistical systems could be used to evade rules.</p></div></section>
<section class="decade1940-return"><div><span>Tour · 2006</span><h2>The winner removed from the result</h2><p>Floyd Landis collapsed one day, attacked from afar the next and won the Tour. A positive test led to disqualification, with Óscar Pereiro named winner.</p></div><div><span>Tour · 2007</span><h2>Yellow did not reach Paris</h2><p>Michael Rasmussen led when Rabobank removed him over false whereabouts information. Danish viewers went to bed with a Tour leader and woke without one.</p></div></section>
<section class="wildwest-copy rust"><div><p class="section-label">After the empty years</p><h2>No single permanent king</h2></div><div><p>Alberto Contador won in 2007, Carlos Sastre decided the 2008 Tour on Alpe d’Huez, and Armstrong returned to finish third in 2009 behind Contador. No replacement winners were named for Armstrong’s annulled victories; the result sheet could no longer tell the whole story.</p></div></section>
<section class="wildwest-copy"><div><p class="section-label">2009</p><h2>The gear cable gets electricity</h2></div><div><p>Shimano’s Dura-Ace 7970 Di2 replaced mechanical cable pull with an electrical signal and motor. It was expensive and batteries on racing bicycles met predictable suspicion, but it neatly ended a decade in which more decisions became data and buttons.</p></div></section>
<section class="period-sources"><p class="section-label">Sources and notes</p><ul><li><a href="https://www.usada.org/athletes/results/u-s-postal-service-pro-cycling-team-investigation/">USADA: US Postal investigation</a></li><li><a href="https://www.shimano.com/en/100th/history/products/14.html">Shimano: Dura-Ace and Di2</a></li></ul></section>
